  "grammar": [
    {
      "title": "Subjunctive vs. Indicative in Complex Clauses",
      "content": "At B1 level, you need to understand when subordinate clauses require the subjunctive vs. the indicative. This depends on the MAIN clause expressing CERTAINTY (indicative) vs. DOUBT, DESIRE, EMOTION, or POSSIBILITY (subjunctive).\n\nKey trigger categories:\n\n**CERTAINTY → INDICATIVE:**\n- creer que (to believe that)\n- es cierto que (it's certain that)\n- es verdad que (it's true that)\n- sé que (I know that)\n- es evidente que (it's evident that)\n\n**DOUBT/NEGATION → SUBJUNCTIVE:**\n- no creer que (to not believe that)\n- dudar que (to doubt that)\n- no es cierto que (it's not certain that)\n- negar que (to deny that)\n\n**DESIRE → SUBJUNCTIVE:**\n- querer que (to want that)\n- desear que (to desire that)\n- pedir que (to ask that)\n- esperar que (to hope that)\n\n**EMOTION → SUBJUNCTIVE:**\n- alegrarse de que (to be happy that)\n- temer que (to fear that)\n- sentir que (to regret that)\n- es importante que (it's important that)\n\n**POSSIBILITY → SUBJUNCTIVE:**\n- es posible que (it's possible that)\n- es probable que (it's probable that)\n\nRule of thumb: If the main clause expresses something SUBJECTIVE (desire, doubt, emotion), the subordinate clause needs subjunctive. If it expresses something OBJECTIVE/FACTUAL, use indicative.\n\nNote: 'es posible que' and 'es probable que' always take subjunctive. 'Es seguro que' takes indicative (certainty) — but 'No es seguro que' takes subjunctive.",
      "table": {
        "headers": [
          "Main Clause",
          "Meaning",
          "Mood"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "creer que",
            "to believe that",
            "indicative"
          ],
          [
            "no creer que",
            "to not believe that",
            "subjunctive"
          ],
          [
            "es cierto que",
            "it's certain that",
            "indicative"
          ],
          [
            "es posible que",
            "it's possible that",
            "subjunctive"
          ],
          [
            "quiero que",
            "I want that",
            "subjunctive"
          ],
          [
            "sé que",
            "I know that",
            "indicative"
          ],
          [
            "temo que",
            "I fear that",
            "subjunctive"
          ],
          [
            "es verdad que",
            "it's true that",
            "indicative"
          ],
          [
            "dudo que",
            "I doubt that",
            "subjunctive"
          ],
          [
            "es importante que",
            "it's important that",
            "subjunctive"
          ]
        ]
      },
      "examples": [
        "Creo que ella viene mañana. (I believe she's coming tomorrow.) — indicative",
        "No creo que ella venga mañana. (I don't believe she's coming tomorrow.) — subjunctive",
        "Espero que tengas razón. (I hope you're right.) — subjunctive",
        "Es posible que llueva. (It's possible it will rain.) — subjunctive",
        "Es cierto que ella viene. (It's certain that she's coming.) — indicative",
        "Es importante que tú estudies más. (It's important that you study more.) — subjunctive",
        "No creo que ellos sepan la verdad. (I don't believe they know the truth.) — subjunctive"
      ],
      "aid_note": "This pattern mirrors Romanian exactly: 'crede că (+indicativ)' vs 'nu cred că (+conjunctiv)'. Use this parallel to your advantage! Romanian speakers already have the conceptual framework — they just need to learn the Spanish trigger verbs."
    }
  ],
